1
00:00:01,380 --> 00:00:07,140
My name is James Leahy, and I'm a principal research scientist at PhysicsX and a mathematician.

2
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:14,670
I'm here to tell you about how we're building foundation models for physics and specifically aerodynamics in this case and automotive aerodynamics.

3
00:00:14,670 --> 00:00:18,750
And we're gonna talk, I'm gonna show you some scaling loss that we're seeing.

4
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:20,190
More data, better models.

5
00:00:20,190 --> 00:00:23,190
It's kind of obvious when you see this happening in language and video.

6
00:00:23,190 --> 00:00:29,820
But for engineering and physics, this is very far from a trivial problem and it's an ongoing effort.

7
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:32,523
And we'll see some early results today.

8
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:36,240
Here's what we're gonna cover.

9
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:39,600
First, I'm gonna give you a quick introduction to who PhysicsX is.

10
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:44,433
Then I'll explain how pre-trained models are the next frontier for physics AI.

11
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:51,270
I'll talk about our new pre-trained model for automotive aerodynamics, and then I'll show you some scaling laws.

12
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:57,040
We'll look at how AWS infrastructure is making this possible and then I'll close with where we're heading next.

13
00:00:59,130 --> 00:01:01,363
So let's start with who PhysicsX is.

14
00:01:02,309 --> 00:01:07,530
PhysicsX is an AI company and we're focused on industrial applications.

15
00:01:07,530 --> 00:01:12,960
Our mission is simple, build and deploy AI to accelerate industrial innovation.

16
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:17,400
We've raised over $155 million in series B funding.

17
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:24,750
We have over 200 engineers, a mix of AI researchers, simulation engineers, and software engineers.

18
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:29,250
I think, crucially, it's important to understand that we're not just a research organization.

19
00:01:29,250 --> 00:01:35,730
We work directly with our customers to build the things that are usable and used.

20
00:01:35,730 --> 00:01:40,170
We partner with leading players across aerospace and defense,

21
00:01:40,170 --> 00:01:46,083
semiconductors, materials, energy and renewables, industrials, and automotive.

22
00:01:49,500 --> 00:02:00,030
So what you're seeing here is a computational fluid dynamic simulation that's approximating the Navier-Stokes equation around an immersed body, in this case, an airplane,

23
00:02:00,030 --> 00:02:04,320
CFD was a major step change from building physical prototypes.

24
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:10,620
Before CFD, if you wanted to know how air flowed around a design, you had to build it, put it in a wind tunnel.

25
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:18,900
Wind tunnels still play a vital role today, but everyone knows that simulations have fundamentally increased the speed at which we can innovate.

26
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:23,643
Simulations though still are a bottleneck, and there's room to innovate faster.

27
00:02:25,650 --> 00:02:27,990
That's where physics AI comes in.

28
00:02:27,990 --> 00:02:30,420
A legacy simulation, like the one you just saw,

29
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:35,610
which was a seven-second transient simulation consisting of over a billion mesh elements,

30
00:02:35,610 --> 00:02:41,640
takes four days to compute Using physics AI, we can do this in a six-second inference step.

31
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:44,970
Four days becomes six seconds in inference.

32
00:02:44,970 --> 00:02:51,903
That's not just an incremental improvement, that's just the fundamental transformation is how manufacturers can innovate and design.

33
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:59,520
The models, they're called surrogates because they act as stand-ins for expensive physics simulations.

34
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:06,290
A well-trained surrogate can give you answers that are accurate enough to make engineering decisions at a fraction of the computational cost.

35
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:11,913
We don't just work on aircrafts.

36
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:17,520
Our technology applies across engineering domains, as I spoke about earlier.

37
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:25,380
Aerodynamics for vehicles, heat transfer for thermal management, chemistry for reactions, and electromagnetics for electronics.

38
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:30,210
The physics is different in each domain, but the fundamental approach is the same.

39
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:36,843
We learn from simulation and hopefully more experimental data and make fast, accurate predictions.

40
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:45,690
So what does it take to build an AI surrogate?

41
00:03:45,690 --> 00:03:52,080
Well, you have to do some of those expensive simulations that I talked about earlier upfront to generate the training data.

42
00:03:52,080 --> 00:04:00,090
You need to store that data, you need to process it, you need to clean it and convert it to formats that ML models can consume.

43
00:04:00,090 --> 00:04:03,450
Then you need to train the models, validate them, and deploy them.

44
00:04:03,450 --> 00:04:10,590
We're building a platform designed to completely support the AI lifecycle for engineering and physics,

45
00:04:10,590 --> 00:04:16,863
the data management, the model training, the fine-tuning, and all deployed as an agentic workflow.

46
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:22,110
We have a simulation workbench which orchestrates the simulations.

47
00:04:22,110 --> 00:04:29,460
We have the AI workbench to develop and deploy models from the AI model catalog, some of which are pre-trained, some of which are not.

48
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:35,790
And then we have a unified data backbone with enterprise security and multi-cloud scalability.

49
00:04:35,790 --> 00:04:40,383
This is not just a research infrastructure, this is production-grade software.

50
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:46,593
So now, let me talk about our pre-trained model for automotive aerodynamics.

51
00:04:47,970 --> 00:04:54,840
So I spoke earlier about how numerical simulation was the first major step change for engineering.

52
00:04:54,840 --> 00:04:57,360
The simulations are complex to set up.

53
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:00,060
They take hours or days.

54
00:05:00,060 --> 00:05:05,220
But you get cheaper and faster exploration than physical prototypes.

55
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:08,100
Then came traditional ML models and surrogates.

56
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:16,620
You need a training dataset, you need an ML model, a pipeline, and once you're trained, you can get vast design space exploration.

57
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:25,530
But for every customer we encounter, every new geometry we see, we have to collect a new dataset from scratch.

58
00:05:25,530 --> 00:05:33,453
So what we're doing is we're going out and generating large pre-training datasets, coupling it with foundational architectures,

59
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:43,050
and serious infrastructure that's involved to get everything from the simulations to the data loader and to train at scale using dynamic data parallelism.

60
00:05:43,050 --> 00:05:54,033
And what you get from these pre-trained foundation models is you get lower upfront data needs for customers and better out-of-the-box performance, zero shot and fine-tuned.

61
00:05:55,590 --> 00:06:07,890
So when we wanted to build the automotive aerodynamics model, we faced some challenges is that there wasn't a very large, diverse corpus of data.

62
00:06:07,890 --> 00:06:15,630
Some of the largest public datasets consists of the DrivAerNet++, which is out of the MIT DeCoDE Lab of Faez Ahmed,

63
00:06:15,630 --> 00:06:23,700
which is a remarkable contribution to the field, make no doubt, that consists of 8,000 Reynolds-averaged Navier-Stokes simulations.

64
00:06:23,700 --> 00:06:26,520
These are steady-state time averaged approximations.

65
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:29,493
They're the fastest amongst the different simulation types.

66
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:38,160
And if you look At though what the data's comprised of, it's eight cars that are morphed,

67
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:41,910
and morphing is like stretching and pulling different parts of the car.

68
00:06:41,910 --> 00:06:46,080
You get very topologically similar cars.

69
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:53,070
Another dataset is a Luminary SHIFT SUV, that's 2,000 detached eddy simulations.

70
00:06:53,070 --> 00:06:56,546
These are of higher fidelity than RANS.

71
00:06:56,546 --> 00:07:01,560
And here there are only two baseline cars that are morphed a thousand times.

72
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:06,420
So that's roughly 10 cars which have a thousand morphs.

73
00:07:06,420 --> 00:07:08,100
It's not very diverse.

74
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:14,660
Another challenge I'll mention is that when you have sort of this diverse data,

75
00:07:14,670 --> 00:07:18,030
you have different fidelity simulations, different turbulence closure models,

76
00:07:18,030 --> 00:07:26,790
and this is sort of still an open research problem as to whether you should pre-train these models on diverse sets of simulations and how exactly you should do it.

77
00:07:26,790 --> 00:07:29,703
We'll only focus on the geometric diversity today.

78
00:07:31,224 --> 00:07:36,420
So okay, I said that these datasets aren't diverse, but why does this matter?

79
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:41,460
So, we pre-trained various attention-based architectures.

80
00:07:41,460 --> 00:07:47,663
If you're familiar, Transolver, ABUPT, point cloud attention, linear attention.

81
00:07:47,663 --> 00:07:53,040
These meshes are very large, so you have to use variants of attention that are suitable.

82
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:58,020
And we trained them on Luminary and DrivAerNet, these two public datasets that I just spoke about.

83
00:07:58,020 --> 00:08:02,160
And we looked at their out-of-distribution performance on a representative customer dataset.

84
00:08:02,160 --> 00:08:03,570
We call it CustomerNet.

85
00:08:03,570 --> 00:08:10,020
That's one baseline simulation with very intricate morphs that we see that our customers have.

86
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:11,430
And we looked at their performance.

87
00:08:11,430 --> 00:08:13,770
What sort of performance are we looking at?

88
00:08:13,770 --> 00:08:16,020
I'll first talk about the quantities that we're looking at.

89
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:17,550
There's the drag coefficient.

90
00:08:17,550 --> 00:08:20,763
This is a measure of the aerodynamic resistance of the car.

91
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:27,240
A typical sedan has around a coefficient of drag between 0.25 and 0.30.

92
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:36,840
Understanding this number is how automotive manufacturers can optimize for fuel efficiency and EV range and other aspects.

93
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:40,890
The pressure coefficient, this is the pressure on the surface of the car.

94
00:08:40,890 --> 00:08:44,700
This tells you how pressure varies across every point of the vehicle surface.

95
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:48,420
You have high pressure region in the front and a low pressure in the wake.

96
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:51,660
And these differences are what create the drag.

97
00:08:51,660 --> 00:08:57,990
And if you can predict the pressure field accurately, you understand why the design has its drag.

98
00:08:57,990 --> 00:09:00,420
So let's look at these graphs.

99
00:09:00,420 --> 00:09:09,060
So if we look at the top one, this is the drag coefficient, and along the y-axis is the Spearman rank correlation coefficient.

100
00:09:09,060 --> 00:09:11,850
It measures how accurate you're ranking the designs.

101
00:09:11,850 --> 00:09:20,580
And you look, in the green and the orange, the green is the model that's trained on DrivAerNet, the orange is the one that's trained on Luminary.

102
00:09:20,580 --> 00:09:25,830
And if you look at the performance within distribution, it's quite good.

103
00:09:25,830 --> 00:09:31,080
If we look at how it performs out of distribution on the CustomerNet, it's orders of magnitude worse.

104
00:09:31,080 --> 00:09:33,240
We see a similar story for the pressure coefficient.

105
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:34,620
Here, lower is better.

106
00:09:34,620 --> 00:09:40,503
We're looking at the mean absolute error of the pressure coefficient on the surface of the car.

107
00:09:42,330 --> 00:09:47,670
So it seems pretty obvious you need to go out and you need to generate a very diverse dataset.

108
00:09:47,670 --> 00:09:51,090
So this dataset is growing as we speak.

109
00:09:51,090 --> 00:09:54,090
We are still generating this data and training the model.

110
00:09:54,090 --> 00:10:00,840
We'll talk a little bit about how we're getting more new, diverser designs through active learning.

111
00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:09,150
So here what you see are some numbers that are trained on 18,000 simulations with over 120 baselines

112
00:10:09,150 --> 00:10:12,330
at a fixed velocity, inlet velocity for now.

113
00:10:12,330 --> 00:10:19,740
And we see a difference here, that our model still performs well on DrivAerNet Luminary,

114
00:10:19,740 --> 00:10:29,310
these public datasets, but when we look at the out-of-shot zero-shot performance, we get significantly better results.

115
00:10:29,310 --> 00:10:30,600
And this is just zero shot.

116
00:10:30,600 --> 00:10:32,100
This isn't when you fine-tune.

117
00:10:33,540 --> 00:10:43,470
So, now you wanna understand, as we're growing this dataset, what's happening out of distribution is your out-of-distribution performance improving.

118
00:10:43,470 --> 00:10:47,830
And so what we're seeing here is a graph along the y-axis is the out of distribution

119
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:51,990
mean absolute error of the pressure coefficient.

120
00:10:51,990 --> 00:10:57,000
And along the x-axis, you see the training set size, and you see this decreasing.

121
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:02,580
Now, when you look along the x-axis, you say training set size, are these just morphs or are these baselines?

122
00:11:02,580 --> 00:11:05,280
And these are some of the questions we're trying to understand.

123
00:11:05,280 --> 00:11:07,770
How many times should you morph a car?

124
00:11:07,770 --> 00:11:09,753
Does that improve the performance?

125
00:11:10,590 --> 00:11:20,790
Here, this is going along basically a hundred morphs per baseline that we're increasing along the x-axis.

126
00:11:20,790 --> 00:11:29,793
And we'll talk about how we're now implementing active learning and that's going to increase the slope of these curves.

127
00:11:31,800 --> 00:11:37,470
So, accuracy alone isn't enough for engineering applications.

128
00:11:37,470 --> 00:11:40,290
Engineers need to know when to trust the model.

129
00:11:40,290 --> 00:11:44,550
If you're predicting a drag coefficient, a difference of 0.01 matters.

130
00:11:44,550 --> 00:11:47,070
You need to have confidence intervals.

131
00:11:47,070 --> 00:11:49,803
And if we look on the right,

132
00:11:51,720 --> 00:11:55,980
this is showing how drag accumulates throughout the length of the car from the front to the back.

133
00:11:55,980 --> 00:12:00,330
You get this by integrating the pressure and the wall shear stress on surface.

134
00:12:00,330 --> 00:12:07,080
And as you get to all the way to the right point of the graph, the final number on the upper right, that's the coefficient of drag.

135
00:12:07,080 --> 00:12:12,210
And you can see that that blue band is the uncertainty region.

136
00:12:12,210 --> 00:12:18,903
And at that final point on the right, that represents the confidence interval around the final drag coefficient.

137
00:12:20,490 --> 00:12:24,990
This is the sort of uncertainty quantification we expect from our models.

138
00:12:24,990 --> 00:12:29,400
And we'll see that this uncertainty quantification isn't just crucial for the end use.

139
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:30,310
You need to know

140
00:12:32,816 --> 00:12:37,140
at which designs the models are uncertain to know which designs to go for next.

141
00:12:37,140 --> 00:12:40,923
And that's the active learning loop that I'll speak about next.

142
00:12:42,630 --> 00:12:51,960
So let me now talk about, we're at an AWS conference, let me talk about how we're using AWS to simulate and train these models.

143
00:12:51,960 --> 00:12:56,250
So let me start with the diverse geometries.

144
00:12:56,250 --> 00:12:59,820
Today, we have a diverse set of geometries.

145
00:12:59,820 --> 00:13:03,360
These then go into our aerodynamic simulation.

146
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:08,370
We're using Siemens star and we're using GPU-based solvers,

147
00:13:08,370 --> 00:13:14,610
and these are on SageMaker HyperPod, the same clusters that our models are being trained on.

148
00:13:14,610 --> 00:13:22,293
So on the output of the aerodynamic simulations, you get the diverse resolve flows, very large set of fields, terabytes upon terabytes.

149
00:13:22,293 --> 00:13:24,333
Those are stored in S3.

150
00:13:25,830 --> 00:13:29,013
That's our long-term storage.

151
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:40,080
Then those fields are then pre-processed using AWS Batch in parallel to get them to a form that's ingested by our models.

152
00:13:40,080 --> 00:13:49,023
Our models are then also trained on SageMaker HyperPod using data parallelism, and the storage that's used there is FSx for Lustre.

153
00:13:50,940 --> 00:13:54,990
And then finally, I talked about that our model has uncertainty quantification.

154
00:13:54,990 --> 00:14:01,920
So that, plus last year at AWS, we spoke about our large generative geometry model.

155
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:04,713
This is a model that can generate different designs.

156
00:14:05,610 --> 00:14:12,210
The uncertainty coupled with the geometry generative mechanism are what drives this active learning loop.

157
00:14:12,210 --> 00:14:16,920
After we had a set of baseline geometries to fine-tune the geometry model,

158
00:14:16,920 --> 00:14:20,670
this can then tell us what parts of the design space is the model most uncertain,

159
00:14:20,670 --> 00:14:26,220
and we can get those geometries which then feed back into the loop to go through the aerodynamic simulation.

160
00:14:26,220 --> 00:14:31,680
Now, that aerodynamic simulation I spoke about was Siemens star.

161
00:14:31,680 --> 00:14:32,880
That's RANS at the moment.

162
00:14:32,880 --> 00:14:35,340
That's not a transient simulation.

163
00:14:35,340 --> 00:14:41,310
I think there's a lot of evidence showing that training on transient data is a better use,

164
00:14:41,310 --> 00:14:45,630
but to train on transient data, you need to have an online data loader.

165
00:14:45,630 --> 00:14:51,600
That's where it becomes even more important that the simulator and the models are trained in the same place.

166
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:56,703
So building this infrastructure even for RANS is setting the stage for the next development.

167
00:14:58,230 --> 00:15:03,510
I'll kind of go over this again in a little more detail about the AWS architecture.

168
00:15:03,510 --> 00:15:06,840
So at the heart is the SageMaker HyperPod.

169
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:08,880
We have a Slurm for job management.

170
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:15,360
We have a controller machine that works across multiple compute nodes and the simulation node.

171
00:15:15,360 --> 00:15:20,310
You have the FSx for Luster, which is the file system layer.

172
00:15:20,310 --> 00:15:24,273
It's bidirectionally synced with S3.

173
00:15:25,470 --> 00:15:28,593
The FSx is where the data loader are.

174
00:15:30,210 --> 00:15:32,877
And then the network architecture is two subnets.

175
00:15:32,877 --> 00:15:37,590
You have the HyperPod, the private one, which is the HyperPod cluster, and you have the public subnet,

176
00:15:37,590 --> 00:15:44,763
which hosts the ML flow dashboard where we see the results, and all of our researchers enter in through the AWS Session Manager.

177
00:15:47,790 --> 00:15:54,213
So let's wrap up and see what we learned and where PhysicsX is heading next.

178
00:15:55,980 --> 00:16:05,730
So we talked about simulations and we saw how ML surrogates were a major leap forward to be able to drive down inference time.

179
00:16:05,730 --> 00:16:12,640
Then we talked about pre-training models and how this will help us

180
00:16:14,220 --> 00:16:19,230
accelerate our customers' workflows by requiring less data from them upfront.

181
00:16:19,230 --> 00:16:22,173
And we're seeing that scaling laws are emerging.

182
00:16:23,460 --> 00:16:25,950
Now, what's next for us?

183
00:16:25,950 --> 00:16:32,280
We started with automotive aerodynamics and we'll continue to innovate here, as I mentioned, going to transient and so on.

184
00:16:32,280 --> 00:16:38,160
But we'll also be looking at other domains, radar cross-section, aeroelastics, structural mechanics.

185
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:41,790
And you should ask, "Well, is this gonna be all one model or are these gonna be separate models?"

186
00:16:41,790 --> 00:16:50,520
Both. We'll train the best model in class and we'll look at what happens when you continue to increase the scope of the model for multiphysics.

187
00:16:50,520 --> 00:16:58,710
I think, we're working with our customers to understand what are the best models for them, and so we'll build both.

188
00:16:58,710 --> 00:17:02,760
I encourage you to visit physicsx.ai to learn more.

189
00:17:02,760 --> 00:17:05,403
We'll be releasing a blog post soon about the model.

190
00:17:06,630 --> 00:17:09,933
And I just want to thank you for your time today and for listening.
